---

<BaseLayout
  title="Striff - Your documented architecture, checked on every pull request"
  description="Striff reads your AGENTS.md, ADRs and architecture docs, turns each sentence about the code into a checkable rule, and answers it on every GitHub pull request, with a diagram of what changed and review notes on the components it touched."
  canonical="https://striff.io/"
  ogImage="https://striff.io/og-image.png"
>
  <main>
    <!-- ==================== HERO ==================== -->
    <!-- The product is a GitHub check, so the hero is one, rendered light and at full
         fidelity. The summary line carries the coverage denominator (the thing nobody
         else can claim) and the rows carry the mechanism: your sentence, the line it is
         on, the rule it became, and what happened to it on this pull request.
         The violated row leads because that is the row a reviewer needs first. -->
    <section class="hero-section relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-[90rem] px-6">
        <div class="hero-copy relative z-10 mx-auto max-w-4xl space-y-5 text-center" data-reveal>
          <h1 class="mx-auto">
            <span class="sr-only">Your architecture docs, enforced on every pull request.</span>
            <span aria-hidden="true">Your <span class="h1-rot" data-h1-rot><span class="h1-rot-word is-on">architecture docs</span><span class="h1-rot-word">design docs</span><span class="h1-rot-word">AGENTS.md</span></span>,<br /> <span class="hero-h1-accent">enforced on every pull request</span>.</span>
          </h1>
          <p class="section-subcopy hero-subcopy mx-auto">
            Striff reads the documents already in your repository, turns the sentences that make claims about the code into rules, and checks every pull request against them. <strong>When a change contradicts what your team wrote down, it says so before the merge, and quotes the line.</strong>
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3 pt-2">
            <a class="btn-primary btn-primary-lg" href={githubAppUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
              <svg class="h-4 w-4" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
                <path d="M8 0C3.58 0 0 3.58 0 8c0 3.54 2.29 6.53 5.47 7.59.4.07.55-.17.55-.38 0-.19-.01-.82-.01-1.49-2.01.37-2.53-.49-2.69-.94-.09-.23-.48-.94-.82-1.13-.28-.15-.68-.52-.01-.53.63-.01 1.08.58 1.23.82.72 1.21 1.87.87 2.33.66.07-.52.28-.87.51-1.07-1.78-.2-3.64-.89-3.64-3.95 0-.87.31-1.59.82-2.15-.08-.2-.36-1.02.08-2.12 0 0 .67-.21 2.2.82.64-.18 1.32-.27 2-.27s1.36.09 2 .27c1.53-1.04 2.2-.82 2.2-.82.44 1.1.16 1.92.08 2.12.51.56.82 1.27.82 2.15 0 3.07-1.87 3.75-3.65 3.95.29.25.54.73.54 1.48 0 1.07-.01 1.93-.01 2.2 0 .21.15.46.55.38A8.01 8.01 0 0 0 16 8c0-4.42-3.58-8-8-8z" />
              </svg>
              Install the GitHub App
            </a>
            <a class="btn-secondary btn-secondary-lg" href="#pipeline">How it works</a>
          </div>
          <p class="hero-microproof">
            <span>Free on public repos</span>
            <span>Nothing to configure</span>
            <span>One check beside your CI</span>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-check-wrap" data-reveal>
          <p class="hero-check-lede">A language model reads your docs and turns their sentences into rules. Each rule is a logical formula, checked against a formal model of your code before and after the pull request. Here is a real run:</p>
          <div class="hero-check-card">
            <header class="hcc-head">
              <span class="hcc-mark"><img src="/icon.svg" alt="" width="20" height="20" /></span>
              <span class="hcc-headings">
                <b>Striff / architectural-review</b>
                <i>Ericsson/ecchronos</i>
              </span>
              <span class="hcc-spacer"></span>
              <a class="hcc-chip hcc-chip-link" href="https://github.com/Ericsson/ecchronos/pull/1786" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PR #1786</a>
              <span class="hcc-chip hcc-chip-muted">Java</span>
            </header>

            <div class="hcc-banner">
              <span class="hcc-banner-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <p class="hcc-banner-text">
                <strong>One of the 63 rules Striff checked in this repository&rsquo;s own docs no longer holds.</strong>
                <span>Nothing was configured. Striff read the repository&rsquo;s documents, turned their sentences into rules, and checked them against both revisions.</span>
              </p>
              <span class="hcc-tally">
                <span class="hcc-tally-item hcc-tally-violated"><b>1</b><i>violated</i></span>
                <span class="hcc-tally-item hcc-tally-held"><b>62</b><i>held</i></span>
              </span>
            </div>

            <RuleRows rows={heroRules} baseSha={heroBaseSha} headSha={heroHeadSha} logicNote />
          </div>
          <div class="hero-langs" aria-label="Supported languages">
            <span class="hero-langs-label">Works with</span>
            {heroLanguages.map((lang) => (
              <span class="lang-chip" style={`--lang: ${lang.color}`}>
                <span class="lang-chip-mark" aria-hidden="true">{lang.mark}</span>
                {lang.name}
              </span>
            ))}
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ==================== PROBLEM ==================== -->
    <section id="problem" class="scroll-mt-24 bg-slate-100/60 pb-16 pt-14">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-[90rem] px-6" data-reveal>
        <div class="rounded-3xl bg-white p-6 shadow-xl border border-slate-200 md:p-8">
          <div class="problem-split">
            <div class="problem-copy">
              <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-rose-500">The problem</p>
              <h2 class="mt-3 text-balance">Nobody re-reads the docs on every pull request.</h2>
              <p class="section-subcopy mt-4">
                Coding agents read your architecture docs as context, then write code that quietly contradicts them, and no reviewer re-reads those documents on every change to catch it. Left unchecked, each merge drifts a little further from what your team agreed, until <strong>every change costs more than it should and nobody wants to touch the core</strong>. Striff catches the contradiction on the pull request, while it is still a one-line fix.
              </p>
              <BlogLink slug="architecture-matters-more-not-less" kicker="Why coding agents make architecture review matter more" class="mt-6" />
            </div>

            <!-- The firehose, run by agents: every lane is an agent's branch and every row in the
                 feed is an agent acting on the repo. -->
            <figure class="gs" data-reveal aria-hidden="true">
              <svg width="0" height="0" style="position:absolute"><defs><linearGradient id="gs-gemini" x1="0" y1="0" x2="1" y2="1"><stop offset="0%" stop-color="#4f8df9" /><stop offset="55%" stop-color="#9b72cb" /><stop offset="100%" stop-color="#d96570" /></linearGradient></defs></svg>
              <div class="gs-head">
                <span class="gs-live"><span class="gs-live-dot"></span><span class="gs-live-repo">5 agents</span> working on acme-corp</span>
                <span class="gs-head-tag">last hour</span>
              </div>
              <div class="gs-body">
                <div class="gs-col">
                  <span class="gs-col-head">agent branches → main</span>
                  <div class="gs-graph">
                  <div class="gs-graph-scroll">
                    {[0, 1].map(() => (
                      <svg class="gs-graph-unit" viewBox="0 0 140 320" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet">
                        <path class="gs-line gs-line-main" d="M30 0 L30 320" />
                        <path class="gs-line gs-line-a" d="M30 256 C 52 256 70 242 70 218 L 70 172 C 70 148 52 144 30 144" />
                        <path class="gs-line gs-line-b" d="M30 224 C 60 224 104 206 104 178 L 104 92 C 104 66 60 64 30 64" />
                        <circle class="gs-dot gs-dot-main" cx="30" cy="32" r="5" />
                        <circle class="gs-dot gs-dot-main" cx="30" cy="96" r="5" />
                        <circle class="gs-dot gs-dot-main" cx="30" cy="160" r="5" />
                        <circle class="gs-dot gs-dot-main" cx="30" cy="224" r="5" />
                        <circle class="gs-dot gs-dot-main" cx="30" cy="288" r="5" />
                        <g class="gs-agent gs-agent-a"><circle cx="70" cy="196" r="9.5" class="gs-agent-bg" /><g transform="translate(64 190) scale(0.5)"><g class="gs-agent-mark"><path d="M11.04 19.32Q12 21.51 12 24q0-2.49.93-4.68.96-2.19 2.58-3.81t3.81-2.55Q21.51 12 24 12q-2.49 0-4.68-.93a12.3 12.3 0 0 1-3.81-2.58 12.3 12.3 0 0 1-2.58-3.81Q12 2.49 12 0q0 2.49-.96 4.68-.93 2.19-2.55 3.81a12.3 12.3 0 0 1-3.81 2.58Q2.49 12 0 12q2.49 0 4.68.96 2.19.93 3.81 2.55t2.55 3.81" /></g></g></g>
                        <g class="gs-agent gs-agent-b"><circle cx="104" cy="168" r="9.5" class="gs-agent-bg" /><g transform="translate(98 162) scale(0.5)"><g class="gs-agent-mark"><path d="m4.7144 15.9555 4.7174-2.6471.079-.2307-.079-.1275h-.2307l-.7893-.0486-2.6956-.0729-2.3375-.0971-2.2646-.1214-.5707-.1215-.5343-.7042.0546-.3522.4797-.3218.686.0608 1.5179.1032 2.2767.1578 1.6514.0972 2.4468.255h.3886l.0546-.1579-.1336-.0971-.1032-.0972L6.973 9.8356l-2.55-1.6879-1.3356-.9714-.7225-.4918-.3643-.4614-.1578-1.0078.6557-.7225.8803.0607.2246.0607.8925.686 1.9064 1.4754 2.4893 1.8336.3643.3035.1457-.1032.0182-.0728-.164-.2733-1.3539-2.4467-1.445-2.4893-.6435-1.032-.17-.6194c-.0607-.255-.1032-.4674-.1032-.7285L6.287.1335 6.6997 0l.9957.1336.419.3642.6192 1.4147 1.0018 2.2282 1.5543 3.0296.4553.8985.2429.8318.091.255h.1579v-.1457l.1275-1.706.2368-2.0947.2307-2.6957.0789-.7589.3764-.9107.7468-.4918.5828.2793.4797.686-.0668.4433-.2853 1.8517-.5586 2.9021-.3643 1.9429h.2125l.2429-.2429.9835-1.3053 1.6514-2.0643.7286-.8196.85-.9046.5464-.4311h1.0321l.759 1.1293-.34 1.1657-1.0625 1.3478-.8804 1.1414-1.2628 1.7-.7893 1.36.0729.1093.1882-.0183 2.8535-.607 1.5421-.2794 1.8396-.3157.8318.3886.091.3946-.3278.8075-1.967.4857-2.3072.4614-3.4364.8136-.0425.0304.0486.0607 1.5482.1457.6618.0364h1.621l3.0175.2247.7892.522.4736.6376-.079.4857-1.2142.6193-1.6393-.3886-3.825-.9107-1.3113-.3279h-.1822v.1093l1.0929 1.0686 2.0035 1.8092 2.5075 2.3314.1275.5768-.3218.4554-.34-.0486-2.2039-1.6575-.85-.7468-1.9246-1.621h-.1275v.17l.4432.6496 2.3436 3.5214.1214 1.0807-.17.3521-.6071.2125-.6679-.1214-1.3721-1.9246L14.38 17.959l-1.1414-1.9428-.1397.079-.674 7.2552-.3156.3703-.7286.2793-.6071-.4614-.3218-.7468.3218-1.4753.3886-1.9246.3157-1.53.2853-1.9004.17-.6314-.0121-.0425-.1397.0182-1.4328 1.9672-2.1796 2.9446-1.7243 1.8456-.4128.164-.7164-.3704.0667-.6618.4008-.5889 2.386-3.0357 1.4389-1.882.929-1.0868-.0062-.1579h-.0546l-6.3385 4.1164-1.1293.1457-.4857-.4554.0608-.7467.2307-.2429 1.9064-1.3114Z" /></g></g></g>
                        <circle class="gs-dot gs-dot-b" cx="104" cy="102" r="4.5" />
                        <circle class="gs-merge gs-merge-a" cx="30" cy="144" r="6.5" />
                        <circle class="gs-merge gs-merge-b" cx="30" cy="64" r="6.5" />
                      </svg>
                    ))}
                  </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <div class="gs-col gs-col-feed">
                  <span class="gs-col-head">activity</span>
                  <div class="gs-feed">
                  <div class="gs-feed-scroll">
                    {[0, 1].map(() => activityFeed.map((a) => (
                      <div class={`gs-item ${agentClass[a.agent]}`}>
                        <span class="gs-item-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d={agentIcon[a.agent]} /></svg></span>
                        <span class="gs-item-text"><b>{agentName[a.agent]}</b> {a.text}</span>
                        <span class="gs-item-meta">{a.time}</span>
                      </div>
                    )))}
                  </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <figcaption class="gs-caption">They all read your architecture docs. <strong>Nothing checks that they followed them.</strong></figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ==================== HOW IT IS COMPUTED, AND WHAT COMES OUT ==================== -->
    <!-- The pipeline and the artifact it produces, side by side: the mechanism answers
         where a number came from, the check answers what you actually get. -->
    <section id="pipeline" class="pipeline-section scroll-mt-24 pb-20 pt-20">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-[90rem] px-6">
        <!-- The heading lives in the left column rather than above both, so the report
             starts level with the top of the section instead of below a paragraph. -->
        <div class="compute-split">
          <div class="compute-col" data-reveal>
            <div class="compute-intro">
              <h2 class="text-balance">Every verdict is computed, not generated.</h2>
              <p class="section-subcopy mt-4">
                Striff is neurosymbolic: a language model reads your docs and proposes rules, and a program decides what is true. A parser turns your code before and after the pull request into a formal model of its types, members and references, resolved the way a compiler does, and each rule becomes a formula in first-order logic. <strong>Every verdict is formal model checking: does the model satisfy the formula?</strong> The language model only reads the prose and explains the results, and nothing is needed beyond the documents your repository already has.
              </p>
            </div>
            <p class="compute-label"><span>01</span> How a finding is produced</p>
            <ol class="pipeline-flow">
              {pipeline.map((step, i) => (
                <li class={`pipe-step pipe-${step.kind}`}>
                  <span class="pipe-rail" aria-hidden="true"><span class="pipe-dot"></span></span>
                  <div class="pipe-body">
                    <p class="pipe-head">
                      <span class="pipe-num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
                      <span class="pipe-name">{step.name}</span>
                      <span class="pipe-kind">{step.kind === "neural" ? "Language model" : "Deterministic"}</span>
                    </p>
                    <p class="pipe-text">{step.body}</p>
                  </div>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ol>
            <BlogLink slug="grounded-ai-findings" kicker="How we keep the model honest" class="compute-invite" />
          </div>

          <div class="compute-col compute-col-report" id="report" data-reveal>
            <p class="compute-label"><span>02</span> What your reviewer sees</p>
            <div class="hero-stage-shell report-stage-shell">
              <div class="ghc ghc-solo" data-report-top>
                <section class="ghc-pane">
                  <div class="ghc-md">
                    <h3>Review Summary</h3>
                    <p>This PR moves repair-history setup into the incremental path. <code>NodeWorker</code> stops referencing <code>RepairScheduler</code> and the table lookups it used, and now depends on <code>SchemaRefresher</code>. <code>RepairHistory</code> drops a public <code>newSession</code> overload that 11 components reach for.</p>

                    <div class="ghc-stack">
                      <section>
                        <h3>Top Review Items</h3>
                        <ul>
                          <li><strong>📄 Doc conflict</strong>: <code>NodeWorker</code> no longer depends on <code>RepairScheduler</code>, which <code>core.impl/README.md</code> line 136 says it calls. It did at the base revision.</li>
                        </ul>
                      </section>

                      <section>
                        <h3 class="ghc-h3-lead">Documented Rules <span class="ghc-h3-meta">63 rules checked &middot; 62 held &middot; 1 violated</span></h3>
                        <ul class="rulelist">
                          {reportRules.map((r) => (
                            <li class={`rulerow rulerow-${r.verdict}`}>
                              <span class="rulerow-main">
                                <InlineCodeText class="rulerow-quote" text={r.quote} />
                                <InlineCodeText class="rulerow-stmt" text={r.statement} />
                              </span>
                              <span class="rulerow-side">
                                <span class={`rulerow-verdict rulerow-verdict-${r.verdict}`}>{r.label}</span>
                                <span class="rulerow-src">{r.doc}<b>:{r.line}</b></span>
                              </span>
                            </li>
                          ))}
                        </ul>
                        <p class="rulelist-more">4 of 63 shown</p>
                      </section>
                    </div>
                    <p>The check also carries the architecture diagram of this change, <a href="#diagram">shown below</a>.</p>
                  </div>
                </section>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ==================== THE DIAGRAM ==================== -->
    <!-- The structural diff, kept exactly as it was but no longer opening the page. The
         rules are what nobody else does; this is what the same check draws beside them, and
         it earns a full stage of its own here rather than competing with the headline. -->
    <section id="diagram" class="diagram-section scroll-mt-24 pb-16 pt-16">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-[90rem] px-6">
        <div class="diagram-intro max-w-3xl" data-reveal>
          <p class="examples-kicker">The diagram</p>
          <h2 class="mt-3 text-balance">Every pull request gets a structural diff diagram.</h2>
          <p class="section-subcopy mt-4">
            The classes a change touched, what each one gained and lost, and how they connect at both revisions, so you can see the shape of a change you didn&rsquo;t write. <strong>Green was added, red was removed and blue changed.</strong>
          </p>
          <a class="oss-link" href="https://github.com/hadi-technology/striff-lib" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 0C3.58 0 0 3.58 0 8c0 3.54 2.29 6.53 5.47 7.59.4.07.55-.17.55-.38 0-.19-.01-.82-.01-1.49-2.01.37-2.53-.49-2.69-.94-.09-.23-.48-.94-.82-1.13-.28-.15-.68-.52-.01-.53.63-.01 1.08.58 1.23.82.72 1.21 1.87.87 2.33.66.07-.52.28-.87.51-1.07-1.78-.2-3.64-.89-3.64-3.95 0-.87.31-1.59.82-2.15-.08-.2-.36-1.02.08-2.12 0 0 .67-.21 2.2.82.64-.18 1.32-.27 2-.27s1.36.09 2 .27c1.53-1.04 2.2-.82 2.2-.82.44 1.1.16 1.92.08 2.12.51.56.82 1.27.82 2.15 0 3.07-1.87 3.75-3.65 3.95.29.25.54.73.54 1.48 0 1.07-.01 1.93-.01 2.2 0 .21.15.46.55.38A8.01 8.01 0 0 0 16 8c0-4.42-3.58-8-8-8z"/></svg>
            <span>The diagram engine is open source: <b>striff-lib</b> on GitHub</span>
            <em>MIT</em>
          </a>
          <BlogLink slug="design-docs-are-enforceable-now" kicker="What this pull request broke, and why it mattered" class="mt-6" />
        </div>

        <div class="relative z-10 mx-auto mt-4 w-full max-w-[90rem]" data-reveal>
          <div class="hero-stage-shell" data-hero-stage>
            <div class="hero-stage-head">
              <div class="hero-stage-context">
                <span class="hero-context-chip">Ericsson/ecchronos</span>
                <span class="hero-context-chip hero-context-chip-muted">PR #1786</span>
                <span class="hero-context-chip hero-context-chip-muted">Java</span>
                <span class="hero-context-chip hero-context-chip-danger">1 rule broken</span>
              </div>
              <div class="hero-stage-actions">
                <div class="hero-stage-badge">Striff / architectural-review</div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="hero-diagram-wrap">
              <!-- Striff's focused diagram of the pull request: the classes it touched around
                   RepairGroup and only the relationships among them. Desktop shows the whole
                   diagram in the band left of the findings overlay (~416px); tablets start at the
                   RepairGroup chain; phones get a crop centred on RepairGroup that pans sideways.
                   Each crop is a view fragment on the image URL, and the stage CSS keeps each image
                   box at its fragment's aspect: re-tune both together. -->
              <div
                class="github-diff-stage example-diff-stage hero-diagram-stage is-static"
              >
                <div class="hero-diagram-scroll">
                  <picture>
                    <source media="(max-width: 767.98px)" srcset="/examples/ecchronos-1786-focused.svg#svgView(viewBox(440,440,1250,605))" />
                    <img
                      src="/examples/ecchronos-1786-focused.svg#svgView(viewBox(-12,-22,2428,1230))"
                      alt="Striff's focused diagram of Ericsson/ecchronos PR #1786: IncrementalRepairJob leading to RepairGroup and on to IncrementalRepairTask and VnodeRepairTask, beside NodeWorkerManager, with added members in green, removed ones in red and changed ones in blue"
                      class="github-diff-graphic github-diff-image"
                      loading="eager"
                      draggable="false"
                    />
                  </picture>
                </div>
                <script is:inline>
                  // Phones: open the sideways-panning crop centred on RepairGroup (x 834-1250 in the
                  // render; the crop starts at x 440 and is 1250 wide).
                  (function () {
